    {
      "id": "ledger-notes",
      "kind": {
        "text": "14 of 90 rows, 15.6%. Kept in the ledger precisely BECAUSE the ratio is worse than the roster's: a lane that reported only its best instance would be selecting on the outcome. The two shapes are the two things an IIS can be -- a local collision of a handful of independent rows (roster, 4.9%) and a global counting argument whose every participant is necessary (here, 15.6%) -- and a consumer choosing between an IIS and a commercial one should know both exist. WHY `search-certificate`: see F:roster-icu-night-iis, same reasoning.",
        "type": "prose"
      },
